//? Title: SRT: actual ready-job decisions
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A single CPU must finish jobs that arrive over time, with known integral burst
lengths. Preemption lets a shorter newly arrived job displace unfinished work.
We expose actual decisions, retain the original four-process workload, and
distinguish scheduling assumptions from unsupported universal optimality claims.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For job i, turnaround is completion minus arrival; waiting subtracts its
required burst:

$$ T_i=C_i-a_i,\qquad W_i=C_i-a_i-b_i.$$
\alttext(Turnaround is completion minus arrival and waiting is turnaround minus required execution time.)

SRT chooses the least remaining work at each arrival/completion. Between events,
running the selected job only decreases its remaining work, so no other ready
job becomes preferable. This proves event-driven intervals reproduce unit ticks.
With known sizes, no switch cost, one CPU and permitted preemption, the standard
interchange argument gives minimum total completion/flow time; arrival and burst
sums are fixed, so total waiting follows. It is not a prediction guarantee with
estimated bursts or switching costs.

Ties use earlier arrival, then original input position, preserving the old
code's actual behavior rather than its inaccurate numeric-ID wording. Idle gaps
jump straight to the next arrival. The implementation scans every input row per
decision, so worst-case work is quadratic in job count; optional copied
remaining-work states are also quadratic. Busy segments merge only when the same
ID runs contiguously. All intervals are half open.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/shortest_remaining_time_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/shortest_first.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::shortest_remaining_time_trace({{9, 0, 3}, {4, 1, 1}});
    std::print("| id | completion | turnaround | wait |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &m : r.schedule.metrics)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", m.id, m.completion, m.turnaround,
                   m.waiting);
    std::print("\n| time | end | idle | chosen id | remaining before |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- " "|\n");
    for (const auto &d : r.decisions)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | `{}` |\n", d.time, d.end, d.idle,
                   d.id, d.remaining);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Jobs: ID9 (0,3), ID4 (1,1)");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual intervals", r.dispatches));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Mean wait {}", r.schedule.avg_waiting));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/shortest_remaining_time_tiny.svg");
}
```
