> API and interpretation

Checked inputs require unique IDs, nonnegative arrival and strictly positive
burst. Empty input has empty timeline and zero means. The complete conservative
horizon max(arrival)+sum(burst) must fit int; huge idle periods do not cause
tick loops. Scan/history policies throw explicitly. The finite static workload
finishes; an open stream of short jobs can starve long jobs. No measured
operating-system overhead is modeled.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Schrage (1968), shortest remaining processing time optimality|https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.16.3.687]

